h training example.\n\n**Example Calculation:**\n\nSuppose you have a neural network with a single hidden layer and a single output layer. The loss function \\( L \\) is the mean squared error (MSE):\n\\[\nL = \\frac{1}{2m} \\sum_{i=1}^{m} (y_i - \\hat{y}_i)^2\n\\]\nwhere:\n- \\( m \\) is the number of training examples.\n- \\( y_i \\) is the true value of the \\( i \\)-th training example.\n- \\( \\hat{y}_i \\) is the predicted value of the \\( i \\)-th training example.\n\nTo compute the gradient of the loss function with respect to a weight \\( w \\), you need to compute:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w} = \\sum_{i=1}^{m} \\frac{\\partial L_i}{\\partial w}\n\\]\n\nThe partial derivative of the loss function with respect to a weight \\( w \\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial L_i}{\\partial w} = (y_i - \\hat{y}_i) \\cdot \\frac{\\partial \\hat{y}_i}{\\partial w}\n\\]\n\nUsing the chain rule, the derivative of the predicted value \\( \\hat{y}_i \\) with respect to the weight \\( w \\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\partial \\hat{y}_i}{\\partial w} = \\sum_{j=1}^{n} \\frac{\\partial \\hat{y}_j}{\\partial w_j} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial w_j}{\\partial w}\n\\]\n\nIn this example, the distributive property is used to simplify the computation of the gradient by breaking it down into the sum of the gradients for each training example.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That example in machine learning context is very helpful. Thank you for explaining it clearly. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the example in machine learning context helpful. The distributive property can indeed be applied in certain scenarios within machine learning, particularly in the context of backpropagation and gradient calculations. If you have any more questions or need further explanations, don't hesitate to ask. I'm here to help! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide an example where the distributive property is used in a quan