ngle \\right) \\otimes \\left( \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |0\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |1\\rangle \\right)\n\\]\n\nUsing the distributive property:\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |00\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |01\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |10\\rangle + \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} |11\\rangle\n\\]\n\\[\n|\\psi\\rangle = \\frac{1}{2} |00\\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} |01\\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} |10\\rangle + \\frac{1}{2} |11\\rangle\n\\]\n\nIn this example, the distributive property is used to simplify the calculation of the state vector of the two-qubit system by breaking it down into the sum of the tensor products of the individual state vectors.\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That example in quantum computing context is very helpful. Thank you for explaining it clearly. <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! I'm glad you found the example in quantum computing context helpful. The distributive property can indeed be applied in certain scenarios within quantum computing, particularly in the context of state vector calculations. If you have any more questions or need further explanations, don't hesitate to ask. I'm here to help! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide an example where the distributive property is used in a game theory context, like calculating Nash equilibria? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>\nCertainly! The distributive property is not typically used directly in calculating Nash equilibria in game theory, but it can be indirectly applied in certain scenarios, especially when dealing with summations and simplifying expressions in the context of mixed strategies.\n\n**Example in Game Theory Context:**\n\nConsider a two-player game where each player has a set of strategies. Let's denote the strategies for Player 1 as \\( S_1 = \\{s_{11}, s_{12}\\} \\) and for Player 2 as \\( S_2 = \\{s_{21}, s_{22}\\} \\). The payoff matrix for the game is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n & s_{21} & s_{22} \\\\\n\\hline\ns_{11} & a_{11} & a_{12} \\\\\ns_{12} & a_{21} & a_{22} \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nTo find the Nash equilibrium, we need to determine the mixed strategies for each player such that no player can benefit by unilaterally changing their strategy.\n\nLet \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) be the probabilities that Player 1 and Player 2 choose strategies \\( s_{11} \\) and \\( s_{12} \\) respectively. The expected payoffs for each player can be expressed as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Expected payoff for Player 1} = p_2 a_{11} + (1 - p_2) a_{21}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Expected payoff for Player 2} = p_1 a_{11} + (1 - p_1) a_{12}\n\\]\n\nFor a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium, the expected payoffs must be equal to the best responses of each player. Therefore, we set up the following equations:\n\n\\[\np_2 a_{11} + (1 - p_2) a_{21} = \\max_{s_{11}, s_{12}} (p_2 a_{11} + (1 - p_2) a_{21})\n\\]\n\\[\np_1 a_{11} + (1 - p_1) a_{12} = \\max_{s_{21}, s_{22}} (p_1 a_{11} + (1 - p_1) a_{12})\n\\]\n\nTo simplify these equations, we can use the distributive property of summation over addition. For example, consider the expected payoff for Player 1:\n\n\\[\np_2 a_{11} + (1 - p_2) a_{21} = p_2 a_{11} + a_{21} - p_2 a_{21}\n\\]\n\nThis expression shows that the expected payoff for Player 1 is the sum of the products of the probabilities and payoffs, which can be simplified using the distributive property.\n\n**Example Calculation:**\n\nSuppose the payoff matrix is:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|cc}\n & s_{21} & s_{22} \\\\\n\\hline\ns_{11} & 3 & 1 \\\\\ns_{12} & 2 & 4 \\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nLet \\( p_1 \\) and \\( p_2 \\) be the probabilities that Player 1 and Player 2 choose strategies \\( s_{11} \\) and \\( s_{12} \\) respectively. The expected payoffs are:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Expected payoff for Player 1} = p_2 \\cdot 3 + (1 - p_2) \\cdot 2 = 3p_2 + 2 - 2p_2 = p_2 + 2\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Expected payoff for Player 2}