we also arrive at the same product:\n\\[\n141 \\times 636 = 88676\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the product of 141 and 636 is indeed 88,676. <|User|>That's really helpful. Can you also show me how to solve this using the long multiplication method?\n<|Assistant|>\nCertainly! The long multiplication method is a step-by-step process that breaks down the multiplication into smaller, manageable parts. Let's solve \\(141 \\times 636\\) using the long multiplication method.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. **Write the numbers one below the other:**\n   ```\n     141\n   \u00d7 636\n   ```\n\n2. **Multiply 141 by 6 (the units digit of 636):**\n   ```\n     141\n   \u00d7   6\n   -----\n     846\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply 141 by 30 (the tens digit of 636, shifted one place to the left):**\n   ```\n     141\n   \u00d7  30\n   -----\n    4230\n   ```\n\n4. **Multiply 141 by 600 (the hundreds digit of 636, shifted two places to the left):**\n   ```\n     141\n   \u00d7 600\n   -----\n   84600\n   ```\n\n5. **Add the results of these multiplications:**\n   ```\n     846\n   +4230\n   +84600\n   -----\n   88676\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of 141 and 636 is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{88676}\n\\]\n\nThis confirms that the product is indeed 88,676. <|User|>This is great! Can you also show me how to solve this using the area model method?\n<|Assistant|>\nCertainly! The area model method is a visual way to break down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. Let's solve \\(141 \\times 636\\) using the area model method.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values:**\n   - \\(141 = 100 + 40 + 1\\)\n   - \\(636 = 600 + 30 + 6\\)\n\n2. **Draw a rectangle and divide it into smaller rectangles based on the place values:**\n   ```\n   +100 | +40 | +1\n   +600 | +30 | +6\n   ```\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**\n   - \\(100 \\times 600 = 60000\\)\n   - \\(100 \\times 30 = 3000\\)\n   - \\(100 \\times 6 = 600\\)\n   - \\(40 \\times 600 = 24000\\)\n   