ep by step:\n1. Multiply the base by the height:\n\\[ 12 \\times 8 = 96 \\]\n2. Multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\times 96 = 48 \\]\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is 48 square units.\n...\n<answer>\nTo find the area of a triangle with a base of 12 units and a height of 8 units, we use the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} \\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times 8 \\]\nFirst, we multiply the base by the height:\n\\[ 12 \\times 8 = 96 \\]\nThen, we multiply the result by \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\times 96 = 48 \\]\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is 48 square units.\n</answer>\nThe area of the triangle with a base of 12 units and a height of 8 units is 48 square units. If you have another problem or question, feel free to ask! <|User|>That was very clear. You made the process easy to understand. Let's try one more: If a rectangle has a length of 15 units and a width of 10 units, what is its area? Please reason step by step. <|User|>Certainly! To find the area of a rectangle, we use the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\]\nGiven that the length is 15 units and the width is 10 units, we can substitute these values into the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 15 \\times 10 \\]\nNow, let's perform the multiplication step by step:\n1. Multiply the length by the width:\n\\[ 15 \\times 10 = 150 \\]\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is 150 square units.\n...\n<answer>\nTo find the area of a rectangle with a length of 15 units and a width of 10 units, we use the formula:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\]\nSubstituting the given values:\n\\[ \\text{Area} = 15 \\times 10 \\]\nFirst, we multiply the length by the width:\n\\[ 15 \\times 10 = 150 \\]\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is 150 square units.\n</answer>\nThe area of the rectangle with a length of 15 units and