\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 720 and 84:\n     - First division: \\( 84 \\div 12 = 7 \\) with a remainder of \\( 84 - 12 \\times 7 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 720 and 84 is 12.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{LCM}(720, 84) = \\frac{|720 \\times 84|}{\\text{GCD}(720, 84)} = \\frac{60480}{12} = 5040\n     \\]\n\n8. **Find the LCM of the result (5040) and the ninth number (90)**:\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 5040 and 90:\n     - First division: \\( 90 \\div 12 = 7 \\) with a remainder of \\( 90 - 12 \\times 7 = 6 \\).\n     - Second division: \\( 12 \\div 6 = 2 \\) with a remainder of \\( 12 - 6 \\times 2 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 5040 and 90 is 6.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{LCM}(5040, 90) = \\frac{|5040 \\times 90|}{\\text{GCD}(5040, 90)} = \\frac{453600}{6} = 75600\n     \\]\n\n9. **Find the LCM of the result (75600) and the tenth number (108)**:\n   - We can use the relationship between LCM and GCD again.\n   - First, find the GCD of 75600 and 108:\n     - First division: \\( 108 \\div 12 = 9 \\) with a remainder of \\( 108 - 12 \\times 9 = 0 \\).\n     - The GCD of 75600 and 108 is 36.\n   - Now, use the LCM formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{LCM}(75600, 108) = \\frac{|75600 \\times 108|}{\\text{GCD}(75600, 108)} = \\frac{8164800}{36} = 227360\n     \\]\n\n### Summary\n\nBy understanding the standard algorithm for multiplication, we can perform the division steps more efficiently and accurately, leading to the correct LCM. Here is the step-by-step process:\n\n1. **Find the LCM of the first two numbers (12 and 18)**:\n   - \\( 12 \\times 18 = 216 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(12, 18) = 6\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(12, 18) = \\frac{216}{6} = 36\\).\n\n2. **Find the LCM of the result (36) and the third number (24)**:\n   - \\( 36 \\times 24 = 864 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(36, 24) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(36, 24) = \\frac{864}{12} = 72\\).\n\n3. **Find the LCM of the result (72) and the fourth number (36)**:\n   - \\( 72 \\times 36 = 2592 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(72, 36) = 36\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(72, 36) = \\frac{2592}{36} = 72\\).\n\n4. **Find the LCM of the result (72) and the fifth number (48)**:\n   - \\( 72 \\times 48 = 3456 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(72, 48) = 24\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(72, 48) = \\frac{3456}{24} = 144\\).\n\n5. **Find the LCM of the result (144) and the sixth number (60)**:\n   - \\( 144 \\times 60 = 8640 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(144, 60) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(144, 60) = \\frac{8640}{12} = 720\\).\n\n6. **Find the LCM of the result (720) and the seventh number (72)**:\n   - \\( 720 \\times 72 = 51840 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(720, 72) = 72\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(720, 72) = \\frac{51840}{72} = 720\\).\n\n7. **Find the LCM of the result (720) and the eighth number (84)**:\n   - \\( 720 \\times 84 = 60480 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(720, 84) = 12\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(720, 84) = \\frac{60480}{12} = 5040\\).\n\n8. **Find the LCM of the result (5040) and the ninth number (90)**:\n   - \\( 5040 \\times 90 = 453600 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(5040, 90) = 6\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(5040, 90) = \\frac{453600}{6} = 75600\\).\n\n9. **Find the LCM of the result (75600) and the tenth number (108)**:\n   - \\( 75600 \\times 108 = 8164800 \\).\n   - \\(\\text{GCD}(75600, 108) = 36\\).\n   - \\(\\text{LCM}(75600, 108) = \\frac{8164800}{36} = 227360\\).\n\nTherefore, the least common multiple (LCM) of 12, 18, 24, 36, 48, 60, 72, 84, 90, and 108 is \\(\\boxed{227360}\\). <|User|>This explanation is very helpful. Could you provide another example where understanding the standard algorithm helps in solving a more complex problem, like finding the least common multiple (LCM) of eleven numbers?\n<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Finding the least common multiple (LCM) of eleven numbers can be a complex problem, but understanding the standard al